{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1, "contents": [{}], "_data": []}, {"id": 3, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n2le01ob04re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n2le01ob04re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n2le01ob04re2img01.jpg", "text": "<strong>Mike</strong>: So, Paul, what do you usually do in your free time? </br>\n<strong>Paul</strong>: I'm very fond of programming software systems; so, I often look for up-to-date information about programming methods. </br>\n<strong>Mike</strong>: Cool, do you know anything about those programming paradigms that define the programming languages? </br>\n<strong>Paul</strong>: Of course! At the moment, I'm reading a magazine article about declarative programming; I sometimes read magazines that specializes on programming languages. </br>\n<strong>Mike</strong>: Declarative programming? Which paradigm is that one? </br>\n<strong>Paul</strong>: Well, declarative programming is an umbrella term that groups some paradigms different from the imperative programming paradigm; what those alternative paradigms have in common is that they do not describe how the program is going to work, but what the program is going to accomplish. </br>", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n2le01ob04re2img02.jpg", "text": "<strong>Mike</strong>: What other paradigms does the term declarative programming cover? </br>\n<strong>Paul</strong>: Some of the programming paradigms covered by the concept of declarative languages are: constraint programming which executes the program as a set of constraints, or conditions that the problem domain solves by giving a certain value to each variable. There are also the domain-specific languages which are very useful languages without being computationally universal. What those languages have in common is that they don't list commands to perform in a given order, but they give each variable its own meaning; for that reason, we can understand them as isolated declarations. </br>", "time": "55000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n2le01ob04re2img03.jpg", "text": "<strong>Mike</strong>: Cool! I heard that, to some degree, declarative languages simplify the process of programming because they focus on the goal rather than on the process. </br>\n<strong>Paul</strong>: That's true, those languages are context independent; this is, any instructional steps are irrelevant.  Thus, we can use the program in different contexts. </br>\n<strong>Mike</strong>: In my work, I sometimes use declarative languages, such as Absys, Alpha, Datalog, Erlang, SGNAL, and SQL. </br>\n<strong>Paul</strong>: Those are well-known declarative languages; I use them all the time.", "time": "101000"}]}}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "type": "WLC", "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "img_path": "", "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can talk about the declarative languages in software programming.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can list some declarative languages. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can give examples of declarative languages.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "In what cases do you think the contextualization of the program is relevant?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "What online applications do you like using? Why?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": "Declarati