ost_instructions", "contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}]}]}], "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}]}], "type": "sena", "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Declarative languages in Software Programming", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 4, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A1.2", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about declarative languages in Software Programming.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Understand key information about the concepts, principles, and methodologies in simple spoken and written situations.  Talk about programming techniques, use cases, kinds, and sequences in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures.  Identifies general aspects related to Software Programming in short written or spoken conversations about past experiences.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob04re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "well-known", "type": "name"}, {"content": "something that is known or recognized ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Those are well-known declarative languages, I use them all the time.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob04aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "up-to-date", "type": "name"}, {"content": "popular or brand new ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I'm very fond of programming software systems, so I often look for up-to-date information about programming methods.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob04aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "isolated", "type": "name"}, {"content": "alone, apart", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: What those languages have in common is that they don't list commands to perform in a given order, but they give each variable its own meaning; for that reason, we can understand them as isolated declarations.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob04aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "degree", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the measurable status of something or someone", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I heard that, to some degree, declarative languages simplify the process of programming.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob04aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "focus", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to give especial attention or interest to something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I heard that those declarative languages simplify the process of programming, because they focus on the goal  rather than on the process.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob04aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "execute", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to perform an action ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Constraint programming which executes the program as  a set of constraints, or conditions that the problem domain solves by giving a certain value to each variable.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n2le01ob04aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Read about Declarative languages in Software Programming. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write at least five main ideas. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Use the simple present and the frequency adverbs.  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share your ideas with your classmates.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I read about Declarative languages in Software Programming. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write five ideas about Declarative languages in Software Programming. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I use the grammar covered in this lesson. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share my work with my classmates. ", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Adverbs of Frequency", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use the <strong>adverbs of frequency</strong> with the simple present to describe how often or regularly you do something or something happens.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": " The <strong>adverbs of frequency</strong> have different degrees of regularity. Some of them are: Never, occasionally, sometimes, often, usually, and always among others.  Never, for example, means 0% and always 100% of frequency. </br></br>  When you use the adverbs of frequency you need to consider the position of the verbs. This is the structure you use: </br></br>  Subject+ <strong>adverbs of frequency</strong>+ verb+ complement</br></br>   For example: We <strong>always </strong>send information in the morning. </br></br>   But when you have the verb <strong>to be</strong>, this is the structure you use: </br></br>   Subject+<strong>verb to be +adverb of frequency</strong> + complement</br></br>   For example: We <strong>are always</strong> in the classroom at 7: 00 am. </br></br>   For example: </br></br>  I <strong>always</strong> go to study</br> She <strong>usually</strong> uses the computer</br> They <strong>normally</strong> send the information by email</br>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":9,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Frequency</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Adverbs of frequency</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"100%\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Always\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"90%\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Usually\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"70%\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Normally \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"50%\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Often\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"40% \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Sometimes\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"30%\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Occasionally\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"10%\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Seldom\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"0%\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Never \\\"}\"],\"styles\":\"\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "Paul, what do you usually do in your free time? ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "I sometimes read magazine that specialize on programming languages.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Simple present tense to talk about acts and permanent states", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can express permanent states and actions with <strong>the simple present tense</strong>.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You can use <strong>the simple present</strong> to refer to routines, daily events, and actions that you frequently do, or things that usually happen.</br></br>  Example: <strong>They design</strong> innovative programs. </br>               <strong>He designs</strong> software. </br></br>  Notice that you need to include <strong>s/es</strong> to the <strong>third person of singular</strong>; this is, the subject pronouns \u201c<strong>he, she, </strong>and <strong>it</strong>\u201d. You also need the auxiliary verb \u201c<strong>do/does</strong>\u201d for the negative and interrogative form. Remember that when you use the auxiliary verb in the negative and interrogative form, the main verb always keeps its infinitive form. </br></br>  In the following chart you can see the structures.                       ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":4,\"columns\":4,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Present Simple</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Affirmative </strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Negative  </strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Questions</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I/you/we/they\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"I find a new program.</br>  You send an email.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"We <strong>don\u00b4t send </strong>new products.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Do</strong> they <strong>get</strong> products?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"He/she/it\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"She <strong>sells</strong> computers software\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"She <strong>doesn't buy </strong>software. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Does</strong> he <strong>buy</strong> a computer?\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\"\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "What do you usually do in your free time?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "What those alternative paradigms have in common is that they do not describe how the program is going to work, but what the program is going to accomplish. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Declarative programming is an umbrella term that groups some paradigms different from the imperative programming paradigm.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Types of Computer Programmers", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Computer programmers, Games programmers and Web programmers, are professions you need to know when you talk about careers in the computer industry.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<strong> Computer programmers </strong></br> Computer programmers work in several areas. Most computer programming jobs have a bachelor's degree as a minimum education requirement. </br></br>  <strong>Game Programmers</strong></br>  They write computer games and sold them, also they write codes in low-level, so their programs will run faster and more efficiently. Game programmers also write codes that drive the graphics. </br></br>  <strong>Web programmers</strong></br>  Those programmers work on the internet, so they have to use web development languages like HTML, Java, C++, among others. They need to work hard on security, as the internet is a very unsafe place where everybody can enter and hack information.   ", "type": "text"}]}]}